# GitHub Issue Workflow

## Issue-linked work — `wip/` branch (**MANDATORY**)

- **When this applies:** Any work tied to a **numbered GitHub issue** (commits use `Fixes #N` / `Resolves #N` / `Closes #N` / `Refs #N`, TDD red/green evidence is posted on the issue, or the task is explicitly tracked as that issue). Chores/docs **without** an issue are exempt.
- **Branch rule — always:** **All** implementation for that scope happens on **`wip/<issue-slug>`** (e.g. `wip/278-capability-test-hygiene`). **Do not** land issue-scoped implementation by committing **directly** to `next`, `main`, or other integration lines; merge the completed `wip/` branch (or open a PR from it) per repo practice.
- **Worktree:** **Strongly prefer** a **dedicated `git worktree`** for the `wip/` checkout when the change is non-trivial or other agents use `next` — same rationale as the closure flow below (isolation + audit trail).
- **Integration:** After review, merge `wip/` → `next` (or the project’s integration branch), then follow **retention / rename** (`wip/` → `done/`) when using the workflow below.

## Worktree hygiene — commit and push (**MANDATORY**)

Dedicated issue worktrees are **temporary local checkouts** — used across machines and **wiped on machine reset**. They are **not** durable storage; **git remotes are**.

- **After every action** (action = edit or script that makes changes) in a worktree: commit per [commit-after-every-edit.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/commit-after-every-edit.mdc) (one commit per action, with `project.yml` + XcodeGen exception).
- **Before ending a turn or pausing work:** working tree **clean** and **`wip/` branch pushed** (`rtk git push all`) — same bar as [git-push-practices.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/git-push-practices.mdc). **Done = pushed** applies inside worktrees too.
- **Do not** leave uncommitted changes or unpushed commits in a temp worktree expecting to “pick up later on this machine.”
- **Before `rtk git worktree remove`:** confirm the branch is pushed and any merge/rename closure steps are complete (see **Worklist closure** below).

## Comment posting — MANDATORY

- **Default: Taska MCP** (`user-taska` → `add_comment`) for issue comments. Resolve `repo_id` via `list_repos` (sixlayer → that repo’s id). Pass the full Markdown in `body` (Taska supports Markdown; no shell quoting hazards).
- **Also prefer Taska** for other issue mutations when the tool exists (`update_issue`, `set_issue_state`, `create_issue`, etc.).
- **Fallback — `gh`:** only if Taska is unavailable (`needsAuth`, error, or not running). Then use `gh issue comment <N> --body-file <path>` (or a heredoc with a quoted delimiter). Prefer `rtk gh` when that is the project’s wrapper. **Never** use inline `gh … --body "..."` — shells interpret backticks/`$` inside double quotes.
- For `gh issue edit <N>` fallback, use the same **`--body-file`** + **`COMMENT_FILE`** pattern.

## Reusable body file (gh fallback only)

- When using the `gh` fallback, use **one stable path per Cursor host process** under `/tmp`, so the agent **overwrites** the same file instead of creating and deleting unique paths (which can trigger repeated “remove file?” prompts).
- **Path:** `/tmp/cursor-gh-issue-comment-${CURSOR_PID}.md`
  - Prefer **`CURSOR_PID`** when the Cursor host sets it for the agent terminal.
  - If **`CURSOR_PID` is unset**, fall back to **`$$`** (current shell PID): `COMMENT_FILE="/tmp/cursor-gh-issue-comment-${CURSOR_PID:-$$}.md"`
- **Do not delete** that file after posting unless it contains secrets you must clear; the next comment overwrites it.
- Taska comments do **not** need this file — pass Markdown directly to `add_comment`.

## When comments go on the issue (source of truth)

GitHub is the durable record: **post each comment in the same step as the event it documents** — immediately after the failing run (red), immediately after the passing run you are recording (green/refactor), not batched at the end of the session. Do not defer comments until work is “all done”; that falsifies the timeline and undermines the issue as audit trail. Prefer Taska `add_comment`; `gh` only if Taska is down.

## TDD red-phase log on the issue (MANDATORY when issue-driven)

When work maps to a GitHub issue, post the **deliberately red** test evidence to that issue **as soon as** you have that failing run (before green implementation). The comment must include:

- **Timestamp** — UTC ISO 8601 (`date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ`) in the body.
- **How tests were run** — scheme/target, MCP tool or CLI, destination/simulator.
- **Failing test id(s) + short verbatim excerpt** (one screenful at most) — assertion + `file:line`. Don't paste full runs; reference a local log path instead.

Post via Taska `add_comment` with the full Markdown body (safe for backticks/`$`/paths). If falling back to `gh`, build the comment in the **reusable body file** under `/tmp` and use `--body-file` — never inline `gh --body "..."`.

See [strict-tdd-definition.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/strict-tdd-definition.mdc) → Required Evidence → GitHub issue red log.

## Leftovers: name the bucket

When proposing or classifying unfinished work, **say which bucket** — do not use bare “follow-up?” (overloaded; agents escape Done with it).

| Bucket | Meaning | Tracking | Parent Done? |
|---|---|---|---|
| **In scope** | Required for this issue to be done right (peer/API/Goal claim, soft-skips that dodge real proof, incomplete twin of a landed helper, …) | Same issue: expand acceptance if needed; finish on the **same** `wip/<issue-slug>` branch (same worktree) | **Blocked** until finished |
| **Child / sub-issue** | Part of a parent/epic; the slice is still *in* the epic | Open a child issue; link from parent; parent acceptance may checkbox the child | Parent **stays open** until the child is fully done/integrated (merged to `next`, `wip/` retired) **or** parent acceptance no longer requires it. A child is **not** an escape hatch. |
| **Follow-up** | Optional / truly out of scope (“you might also…”, similar pattern elsewhere) | **Own** open issue per item, linked from the parent close comment | Parent may close after land **only if** no in-scope or required-child work remains |

**Prefer finishing in scope** on the parent `wip/` branch. A child issue is fine for visibility/parallelism **only if** the parent stays open and acceptance still requires the work.

Structure (same branch vs child) is flexible; **Done gating is not**.

**No leftover escape:** Do not default to bare “want a follow-up?” for incomplete twin work, thin proof, or soft-skips that dodge the real assertion. Classify as **in scope**, **child / sub-issue**, or **follow-up** — not a bare follow-up question. Twins and soft-skip dodges are **in scope** (same branch, or a child that keeps the parent open) — not optional follow-ups.

**Never** leave leftover bullets only in a closed issue, PR summary, or chat — they will not be seen. Closed issues are not a backlog.

## When to close issues

- **Default:** **Close when the work is done on its branch** — don't wait for merge to `main`. Name the branch in the closing comment (e.g. implemented on **`wip/…`** then merged, or on **`done/…`** after rename). Issue-linked implementation should have lived on **`wip/<issue-slug>`** before integration (see above).
- Merge to `main` is part of the release process, not a prerequisite for closing (unless you are using the integration-line workflow below — then `next` is part of “done”).
- **Leftover gates (before close):** no **in-scope** leftovers remain (or reopen and finish); no open **child** issues that parent acceptance still requires; every **follow-up** has its own open issue, linked from the close comment.

### Worklist closure: `wip/` + `next` + dedicated worktree

When issue-linked work used a **`wip/<issue-slug>`** branch (often in a **temp worktree**) and lands on **`next`** (not necessarily `main`), **do not close the issue until** this sequence is finished; then post the **final** comment and close. If no temp worktree was used, **skip step 4** and state that explicitly in the closing comment.

1. **Merge `next` into `wip/...`** (in the worktree or equivalent checkout), resolve conflicts, commit the merge if needed, push.
2. **Merge `wip/...` into `next`** in the primary clone with **`git merge --no-ff`** (never fast-forward — see [no-fast-forward-merges.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/no-fast-forward-merges.mdc)), push `next` (`git push all next` or repo convention).
3. **Retire** `wip/<slug>` → **`done/<slug>`** with `Development/scripts/retire_wip_branch.sh <slug>` (rename, `git push all done/<slug>`, delete remote `wip/`, remove the temp worktree, **delete the local `done/` branch**). Remotes keep `done/` for history; do not leave an unused local `done/` tip (#459). Leftover local `done/*`: `Development/scripts/repair_branch_upstreams.sh`.
4. **Worktree:** `retire_wip_branch.sh` removes the dedicated worktree. If you retired with `--no-worktree-remove` (or renamed by hand), **`git worktree remove <path>`** now. If no temp worktree was used, **skip** and state that in the closing comment.
5. **Then** finalize GitHub state: confirm leftover gates (no in-scope leftovers; no open required children; each follow-up has its own open issue). Replace the issue body with the **Final Record** (mandatory section below) via Taska `update_issue` (or `gh issue edit --body-file` fallback). Add a comment (Taska `add_comment` / `gh` fallback) documenting steps 1–4: merge SHAs, `next`/`done/` tips, **worktree path removed**, pushes, **leftover issue numbers**. Close via Taska `set_issue_state` (or `gh issue close --reason completed`). **Order:** edits and comment **before** close (close last). If the issue was closed early by mistake, **reopen**, post the closure comment, then close again.

If production work used **`wip/`** + **`next`** but you close before step 5, the audit trail diverges from the integration-line record — avoid that.

## Final record rewrite before close (MANDATORY)

When an issue is ready to close, replace the body with a concise final record (Taska `update_issue`, or `gh issue edit <N> --body-file` fallback) that includes:

- **Scope** — what was in/out of scope.
- **Decisions** — key trade-offs and rationale.
- **Delivered API** — final public interfaces/behaviors shipped (or explicitly "no API changes").
- **Tests** — what was added/updated and current status.
- **Leftovers** — bucketed: in-scope remaining (must be none), required children (none or closed/integrated), follow-ups (each an open issue, linked).
- **References** — PR(s), branch, commit(s), design docs, related issues.

Prefer this structure:

```md
## Final Record

### Scope
- ...

### Decisions
- ...

### Delivered API
- ...

### Tests
- Added/updated: ...
- Result: ...

### Leftovers
- In scope remaining: none
- Required children: none (or #N closed/integrated)
- Follow-ups: #N, #M (open, linked from this close)

### References
- PR: ...
- Branch: ...
- Commits: ...
- Related: ...
```

## Pattern

**Default (Taska):** `list_repos` → `add_comment` / `update_issue` / `set_issue_state` with Markdown `body`.

**Fallback (`gh`) when Taska is unavailable:**

```bash
COMMENT_FILE="/tmp/cursor-gh-issue-comment-${CURSOR_PID:-$$}.md"

cat > "$COMMENT_FILE" <<'EOF'
...body...
EOF
gh issue edit 3 --body-file "$COMMENT_FILE"   # final self-contained record
gh issue comment 3 --body-file "$COMMENT_FILE"
gh issue close 3 --reason completed
```
